<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.1.3: Using Zero Exponent Property and Negative Exponents</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01239</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.1.3: Using Zero Exponent Property and Negative Exponents</md:title>
    <md:uuid>7c6ae07d-bab5-4d10-90ab-c1c4573466a1</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Zero Exponent Property</para>
  <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\) is a non-zero number, then \(a^0=1\).</para>
  <para>If \(a\) is a non-zero number, then \(a\) to the power of zero equals 1.</para>
  <para>Any non-zero number raised to the zero power is 1.</para>
</note>
<para>Complete problems 1 - 6 using the product property for exponents. Double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge.</para>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para> \(d^0\) </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(1\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(7^0\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(1\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((-8)^0\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(1\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\((-v)^0\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(1\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(r^0 \cdot r^0\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(1\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold">Definition of a Negative Exponent</para>
  <hr/>
  <para> If \(n\) is an integer and \(a\neq0\), then \(a^{-n}=\frac{1}{a^n}\).</para>
</note>
<para>Complete problems 6 - 10 using the product property for exponents. Double click on mathematical expressions/equations to enlarge.</para>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(g^{-4}\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(\frac{1}{g^4}\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(2^{-3}\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(\frac{1}{2^3}\) or \(\frac18\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(\frac{1}{c^{-2}}\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(c^2\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(\frac{1}{(-h)^{-6}}\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\((-h)^6\)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>\(\frac{1}{4^{-3}}\)</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\(4^3\) or \(64\)</para>
  </solution></exercise></section>
